c art, showcasing early human expression and creativity."}, {"sourceSlug": "james-melaart", "sourceName": "James Melaart", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "catalhoyuk", "targetName": "Çatalhöyük", "context": "James Melaart's excavations at Çatalhöyük led to the creation of significant archaeological findings, including the Seated Woman."}], "places": [{"name": "Çatalhöyük, Turkey", "role": "Archaeological Site"}, {"name": "Near East", "role": "Cultural Interaction Hub"}, {"name": "Anatolia, Turkey", "role": "Region of Discovery"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Seated Woman of Çatalhöyük", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "seated-woman-of-çatalhöyük"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2233466", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seated_Woman_of_%C3%87atalh%C3%B6y%C3%BCk"], "tags": ["sculpture", "Sculpture & Monuments", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Seated Woman of Çatalhöyük contributed to the historical record of the Prehistoric period. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced artistic traditions and cultural expression. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Seated Woman of Çatalhöyük serves as a critical artifact that illuminates the role of women in early agricultural societies, influencing contemporary understandings of gender in history and art.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-10T05:58:10Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29072308154", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Neolithic sculpture found in turkey Created by http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/8e8edf722ddd258844e31d8140474691. Dating to c. 6000 BCE.", "newValue": "The Seated Woman of Çatalhöyük, a Neolithic figurine discovered at the archaeological site of Çatalhöyük in modern-day Turkey, dates back to approximately 6000 BCE. This artifact is significant as it embodies the artistic and cultural expressions of early agricultural societies and provides insight into the socio-religious dynamics of prehistoric communities. Unearthed during the 1960s by British archaeologist James Melaart, this figurine has captured the attention of historians and archaeologists, symbolizing the connection between gender roles and ritual practices in early human civilizations.

The Seated Woman is a stylized representation of a female figure, often interpreted as a fertility symbol or a representation of a mother goddess. This interpretation is supported by the presence of similar figurines found across various sites in the Near East, suggesting a shared cultural motif among Neolithic peoples. The discovery of the sculpture in the context of a densely populated settlement reveals much about the social structure of Çatalhöyük, where communal living and the practice of agriculture transformed human lifestyles and economic practices around 7000 years ago.

